-- std/secret - Gated secret resolution (M-SECRET-EFFECT, v0.26.0)
--
-- Resolve a secret reference to its value behind approval. The value is labelled <secret>: it cannot reach a {not secret} sink, and execution traces record <redacted:secret> in its place (M-TRACE-LABEL-AWARE) -- except for a value returned from ANOTHER user module (see below).
--
-- Resolution is gated behind a (possibly remote, human) approval. The
-- reference is safe to pass around and log; the resolved value is labelled
-- <secret> (M-TAINT-TYPES) so the type system forbids it from reaching a
-- {not secret} SINK -- a function whose signature demands it, such as a
-- network body -- without an explicit ! {Declassify} step.
--
-- EXECUTION TRACES (M-TRACE-LABEL-AWARE, v0.36.0).
--
-- Tracing runs below the type system, so the label cannot reach the tracer
-- at runtime. Instead the IFC checker marks every call that receives,
-- captures or returns a labelled value, and nothing in that call's dynamic
-- extent records a value: function arguments and results, and effect
-- arguments and results (a request body, a written file), are recorded as
-- <redacted:secret>, at every tier. Names, arity and the span tree are kept.
-- Before v0.36.0 a value a program printed 6 characters of appeared in full
-- 4 times in a deep trace; it now appears 0 times (canary tests in
-- internal/runner/trace_label_redaction_test.go).
--
-- What this does NOT cover yet, stated rather than implied:
--   * A labelled value returned by a function in another USER module. The IFC
--     check is per-module, so the importer sees an unlabelled result -- the
--     same gap as for {not secret} sinks (m-ifc-cross-module-labels). A
--     secret() call is covered in whichever module makes it, but once a
--     library function returns that value to another module, it is not.
--   * The bytecode VM does not carry the marks: a traced run of a labelled
--     program falls back from --bytecode to the evaluator, and
--     --strict-bytecode refuses it.
-- For a guarantee that does not depend on the checker at all, record no
-- values: AILANG_TRACE_VALUES=off (see docs/docs/guides/debugging.md).
--
-- Trust model A: resolution runs on the server via the 1Password CLI with a
-- service-account token. Approval is pushed to the operator's device.
--
-- Requires: Secret capability (--caps Secret)

module std/secret

-- secret: Resolve an op:// reference to its plaintext value after approval. The result is labelled <secret>: {not secret} sinks reject it and traces record <redacted:secret> instead (M-TRACE-LABEL-AWARE; cross-module caveat in the module header).
-- The result is labelled <secret> (M-TAINT-TYPES): CheckModuleIFC forbids it
-- from reaching a {not secret} sink without an explicit ! {Declassify} step,
-- and every traced call it flows into records no values.
-- `purpose` is the human-readable reason shown in the approval request.
-- Example: let key = secret("op://Prod/stripe/api-key", "charge order #1234")
export func secret(ref: string, purpose: string) -> string<secret> ! {Secret} = _secret_read(ref, purpose)
